 {:seon.task/name "an-armed-call-costs-its-checks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; malli jolt/total-arms-unguarded bf35c354, 26aeb44c). A contracted call cost 354 B over a plain one (seon.cost/units :fn/armed-call, [:=> [:cat :int] :int]). Measured apart: the profile count 0 B; each arm's check 176 B, the input's and the output's alike, whatever it validated: malli's -instrument wrapped each check in a try (our fork's 56394c54, so a throwing validator is reported as ::invalid-schema-at-call, never as the function's error), and a Jolt try/catch is a one-shot escape continuation and a pushed handler, ~160-176 B per entry, near its floor (scheme-adapter-runtime.ss, jolt-try-body). Fixed in malli, where the cost is made: whether an arm can throw is decided once at instrument time (m/-total?): every node a type that answers for any realized value (the built-ins, malli's own predicate schemas but pos? neg? zero?, which are registered bare and throw on a non-number, its comparators, function schemas when no ::function-checker is set, and the types options name in ::total-types), named references followed; a :ref (lazy), a :fn or a user's own simple schema keeps its try. A lazy seq an argument holds can still throw while a sequence check realizes it: that throw is the caller's, as when the function reads it. Seon names its predicate pack total (seon.contract/options). Measured: an armed call 352 -> 0 B (the unit table's row: 12.2 -> 10.2 ticks; a bare -instrument of both arms, without the count: 8 -> 6); a call of [:=> [:cat :map :int] [:map]] 512 -> 144 B; seon.source/import-tx of src and test warm 2,193 -> 1,990 MB (-9%), ticks unchanged. The unit table's :fn/armed-call row re-measured."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current
                      seon.contract-test/an-armed-call-costs-its-checks-not-a-try
                      seon.contract-test/an-arm-that-can-throw-is-still-reported-as-the-schemas}}]
